# tests/machine-tools — the commands that answer with THIS MACHINE's state, or act on it.
#
# Read by tests/run-tests, which puts a tripwire in front of each for the whole
# run (and inside every PATH farm), and by devtools/sandbox, which replaces each
# with a decoy. A test that needs one of these stubs it; a call that reaches the
# tripwire is a verdict taken from whatever machine the suite ran on, and fails
# the run.
#
# WRITTEN BY HAND, AND WHY THAT IS ALLOWED HERE. Everything else the suite scans
# is derived (tests/README.md, rule 2). Whether a command reads the machine is a
# property of the command, not of its call sites, so there is nothing to derive
# it from. The backstop is devtools/sandbox: a tool missing from this list is not
# decoyed, but whatever it reads under /sys, /proc, /run, /var/log or
# /var/lib/bluetooth is, and its READ and DATA detectors fire on that.
#
# One name per line; '#' starts a comment.

# systemd and the journal
journalctl
systemctl
systemd-run
systemd-analyze   # `verify` resolves a unit against THIS host's unit directories
systemd-cat
coredumpctl
loginctl
busctl
sudo              # acts as another user: the watchdog hands a notification to a session through it
runuser           # likewise: bt-ui-capture runs pw-metadata and gsettings as the desktop user
timedatectl
hostnamectl
reboot
shutdown
poweroff
halt

# Bluetooth
bluetoothctl
btmgmt
btmon
hciconfig
hcitool
hcidump
hciattach
btattach
rfkill

# devices, modules, the kernel
udevadm
lsusb
modprobe
rmmod
insmod
lsmod             # the kmod family: one binary, and a decoy over one catches all
modinfo
depmod
dmesg
uname

# D-Bus, audio, the desktop session
dbus-send
gdbus
pw-dump
pw-cli
wpctl
pactl
notify-send
wall
logger

# the network
tcpdump
curl
wget
nmcli
iw
gh                # GitHub's API, with the host's credentials. No test reaches it (an exact
                  # trace, 2026-10-09: every gh run was a test's own stub); this keeps it so

# /proc/uptime is read by uptime(1) itself, which no BT_PROC seam can redirect
uptime
